From: Kevin Liu <11365056+kliu128@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 23:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ath11k: nss: keep the Ethernet header on path-not-found
 frames

When the NSS firmware has no mesh path (or proxy path) for a frame it was
asked to encapsulate, it hands the frame back and the driver sends it
through the host transmit path so that mac80211 can resolve the path.
The driver calls eth_type_trans() first, which pulls the Ethernet header,
and then dev_queue_xmit() with skb->data at the network header. mac80211
takes the first twelve payload bytes as destination and source address.

Every such frame is lost, the real destination never gets its path
resolved, and the tables fill with addresses made of IP header fields:
unresolved mesh paths like 60:0a:ee:20:00:3b (IPv6 version, flow label,
payload length) and proxy entries like 40:00:40:06:xx:xx (IPv4 flags, TTL,
protocol, checksum). An IPv4 frame becomes a group-addressed frame (0x45
has the group bit set) and is flooded to every peer.

Seen on TP-Link Deco XE5300 units after a mesh peer restarted: the node
that stayed up could no longer reach addresses behind the restarted one
until it was rebooted itself.

Put the header back before transmitting.

---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/nss.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/nss.c
@@ -2160,6 +2160,10 @@ ath11k_nss_mesh_obj_ext_data_callback(st
 					skb->data, skb->len);
 			skb->protocol = eth_type_trans(skb, dev);
 			skb_reset_network_header(skb);
+			/* eth_type_trans() pulled the Ethernet header; the transmit
+			 * path needs it back, or it reads addresses from the payload.
+			 */
+			skb_push(skb, ETH_HLEN);
 			dev_queue_xmit(skb);
 		break;
 		default:
